<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Recycling for Our World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Composting Is Easy.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give a simple report based on a chart. Interactive: Exchange information about places.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give a simple report based on a chart using clear statements (e.g., "This chart shows how many students are recycling in this class."). Can exchange information about local places (e.g., "In my community, there is a recycling center."). Can ask questions about shared information (e.g., "How many people in this school recycle at home?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f0f0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or realia of items related to composting and recycling (e.g., "food scraps," "plastic bottle," "paper," "compost bin," "recycling bin"). The teacher points to each item and asks, "What is this?" or "Can we compost this?" or "Can we recycle this?" Students respond chorally or individually, activating previously learned vocabulary. (e.g., "Food scraps!" "Yes, we can compost it.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models simple statements and questions using the target grammar and vocabulary. The teacher holds a picture of a recycling center and says, "There is a recycling center in my community. We can recycle plastic and paper there." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /r/ in "recycle" and /s/ in "sustainability" (e.g., "re-cy-cle," "sus-tain-a-bil-i-ty"). The teacher also models asking, "What can we recycle?" and answering, "We can recycle bottles."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with concept-checking questions and examples.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we put food scraps in the recycling bin?" (Expected answer: "No, we put them in the compost bin!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is recycling easy or difficult?" (Expected answer: "It's easy!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple visual aid: a large, colorful bar chart titled "What Our School Recycles." The chart shows categories like "Plastic bottles," "Paper," and "Food scraps" with simple numbers (e.g., "Plastic bottles: 20," "Paper: 30," "Food scraps: 15"). The register is informative, and the format is a basic infographic.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand simple spoken information presented visually in a chart format related to recycling activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher guides students to understand the chart using gist and detail questions.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this chart about?" (Expected: "Our school recycling.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False questions:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Our school recycles 30 plastic bottles." (Teacher points to "Plastic bottles: 20" - Students say: "False!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Our school recycles more paper than plastic bottles." (Students say: "True!").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using Present Simple (e.g., "We recycle paper."), modal verb "can" (e.g., "We can recycle."), and "There is/there are" (e.g., "There is a bin.") to discuss recycling and composting. Target vocabulary includes: plastic, bottles, compost, recycling, trash, food scraps, bin, bag, recycle, help, easy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion.</b> The teacher shows incomplete sentences on the board related to the chart from Stage 2. Students complete the sentences orally.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This chart shows our school ______." (Expected: "recycles")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We can recycle ______, plastic, and ______." (Expected: "paper", "food scraps")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"There is a ______ for paper." (Expected: "bin")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Sentence Builder Drill.</b> The teacher provides a sentence frame and prompts students to substitute words.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "We can recycle paper." (Students repeat.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "plastic bottles." (Students say: "We can recycle plastic bottles.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "food scraps (compost)." (Students say: "We can compost food scraps.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "In my community, there is a recycling center." (Students repeat.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "compost program." (Students say: "In my community, there is a compost program.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: Guessing Game.</b> Students work in pairs. Each student has a small card with a picture of a recycled item (e.g., plastic bottle, newspaper) or a place (e.g., compost bin, recycling center). One student describes their card using "I can recycle...", "There is...", or "It is easy..." without naming it. The partner guesses the item or place. (e.g., "It's plastic. We can recycle it. It's a bottle." Partner: "Plastic bottle!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative information exchange and simple report based on a chart.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure: "Our Community's Green Habits"</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs (A and B). Each pair receives two different, simple charts (Chart A and Chart B). Chart A shows recycling habits of "School A" (e.g., 20 plastic, 15 paper, 5 compost), and Chart B shows "School B" (e.g., 18 plastic, 25 paper, 10 compost). Both charts include a section for "Community Recycling Center: Yes/No" and "Home Composting: Yes/No."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phase 1: Individual Report.</b> Student A looks at Chart A and prepares a simple report about School A's recycling: "This chart shows School A. In School A, they recycle 20 plastic bottles and 15 pieces of paper. They compost 5 food scraps. There is a recycling center in their community." Student B does the same for Chart B.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phase 2: Information Exchange.</b> Student A asks Student B questions about School B and vice-versa to complete missing information on their own chart or to compare:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "How many plastic bottles does School B recycle?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "They recycle 18 plastic bottles."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Is there a recycling center in their community?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Yes, there is."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "What about School A? Do they compost food scraps?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Yes, they compost 5 food scraps."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use phrases like "We can recycle...", "It is easy to compost...", and the target vocabulary (plastic, bottles, compost, recycling center, food scraps, bin, save, help). The teacher monitors for correct pronunciation of /r/ and /s/.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher circulates and observes pairs during the "Our Community's Green Habits" activity, using an observation checklist to note student performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> (e.g., clear /r/ in "recycle", /s/ in "sustainability").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., plastic, bottles, compost, recycling center, food scraps, bin).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of statements:</b> (e.g., "This chart shows...", "In my community, there is...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Ability to ask and answer questions:</b> (e.g., "How many...?", "Is there...?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and turn-taking:</b> (Students interact cooperatively).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers to the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving the class:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to talk about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing I learned to say about recycling?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection, connection to next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about recycling did I use today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I help my community recycle more based on what I said today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the speaking activities? (e.g., The chart-based reporting engaged students, and the pair interaction was lively.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with question formation; more focused practice on "What can we...?" and "How many...?" is needed. Perhaps a review of numbers would also be beneficial.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 4 - Writing):</b> In the next lesson, students will use the information exchanged and reported today to write simple sentences and short paragraphs about their community's recycling and composting efforts. They will construct sentences based on the charts and their discussions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>